    14	
    15	// WorkbenchReport is the fast-authoring-loop entry point (design.md's
    16	// observability addendum, rpg-toolkit#842): compile raw spec bytes at a
    17	// given seed and describe the result as human-readable text, without a
    18	// server. An author edits a YAML file, runs the workbench CLI
    19	// (encounter/cmd/dungeonspec-workbench), and sees the layout in seconds.
    20	//
    21	// A spec that fails to Load (Decode or Validate) still returns a non-empty
    22	// report -- naming the verdict INVALID and the error -- alongside the
    23	// error itself, so a caller (the CLI) always has something to print. A
    24	// spec that Loads successfully is then run through a throwaway
    25	// Encounter.InitDungeon at seed so the report's floor plan reflects the
    26	// SAME wall/door/obstacle generation a real encounter would produce at
    27	// that seed -- not a re-derivation of the compiler's own output -- and
    28	// then through SeedMonsters (no AddPlayer call first: this is a
    29	// throwaway single-report encounter, not a live one, and SeedMonsters'
    30	// combat-entry check is harmless with zero players seated), the third leg
    31	// of the same pipeline Load's own doc names (InitDungeon -> AddPlayer ->
    32	// SeedMonsters). Running it here is not optional: rpg-toolkit#842's gate
    33	// finding was a spec that Loaded and InitDungeon'd cleanly could still
    34	// fail at THIS step, on some fraction of seeds, because a placed
    35	// monster's cell hadn't been reserved from the rolled-obstacle draw --
    36	// without this call, the workbench would report VALID on a spec that
    37	// SeedMonsters actually rejects, exactly the failure mode this tool
    38	// exists to catch before an author ships a spec.
    39	//
    40	// seed 0 is entropy-seeded (matches DungeonParams.RandomSeed's own zero-
    41	// value contract) -- pass a non-zero seed for a reproducible report.
    42	func WorkbenchReport(raw []byte, seed int64) (string, error) {
